{
  id: "rc-y6-01", year: "Y6", genre: "Fiction", title: "The Last Rehearsal",
  text: "On the final afternoon before the performance, the school hall felt different somehow -- charged with a nervous energy that hadn't been there during the previous weeks of rehearsals, when everyone had still been fumbling over lines and missing their cues. Jamal ran through his lines one final time under his breath, though by now the words felt less like memorised text and more like something genuinely his own, something he could say without even thinking about the next line. Across the stage, Mrs Adeyemi watched with an expression he couldn't quite read: pride, certainly, but something else too, something that looked almost like relief. She had spent every lunchtime for two months in this hall, correcting the same mistakes over and over with a patience that never seemed to run out, and tomorrow, all of that preparation would finally mean something real, in front of an audience rather than an empty hall.",
  questions: [
    { type: "mc", skill: "retrieval", q: "What is happening the day before the passage takes place?", options: ["The final performance", "The previous weeks of rehearsals", "A school holiday", "An exam"], correct: 1, evidence: "The passage mentions \"the previous weeks of rehearsals\" happened before this final afternoon." },
    { type: "written", skill: "inference", q: "What might explain the \"something else\" in Mrs Adeyemi's expression, beyond pride?", model: "It could be relief that all her hard work directing the pupils has paid off, or perhaps nervousness about how the actual performance will go.", evidence: "The text hints it \"looked almost like relief\", suggesting the pressure of preparation is finally coming to an end for her too." },
    { type: "mc", skill: "language", q: "What does the phrase \"charged with a nervous energy\" suggest about the atmosphere?", options: ["The hall was completely calm and empty", "There was a tense, excited feeling in the air", "The electricity in the hall was faulty", "Everyone had already left"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"Charged\" is used figuratively here to describe a tense, excited atmosphere, not literal electricity." },
    { type: "written", skill: "summarising", q: "Summarise what this passage tells us about Jamal's feelings towards the play.", model: "Jamal has become genuinely invested in the play, no longer just reciting memorised lines but feeling a real connection to the role he is performing.", evidence: "The text says the words \"felt less like memorised text and more like something genuinely his own.\"" }
  ]
},
{
  id: "rc-y6-02", year: "Y6", genre: "Non-fiction", title: "The Hidden Cost of Fast Fashion",
  text: "Behind the appeal of cheap, trendy clothing lies a complex and often troubling industry, one that rarely gets mentioned on the label attached to a garment. Producing a single cotton T-shirt can require thousands of litres of water, while manufacturing processes frequently release harmful chemicals into rivers near factories, often in countries with far weaker environmental protections than our own. Many garments are worn only a handful of times before being discarded, contributing to enormous quantities of textile waste each year, much of which ends up in landfill rather than being recycled. Increasingly, consumers and campaigners alike are calling for change, encouraging people to buy fewer, longer-lasting items and to consider the true environmental cost hidden behind a low price tag, rather than simply chasing the latest passing trend advertised online, and some campaigners now argue that governments should regulate the whole industry far more closely than they currently do, from water use to worker safety.",
  questions: [
    { type: "mc", skill: "retrieval", q: "What can manufacturing processes release into rivers, according to the passage?", options: ["Clean water", "Harmful chemicals", "Cotton fibres", "Recycled materials"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"Manufacturing processes frequently release harmful chemicals into rivers near factories.\"" },
    { type: "written", skill: "summarising", q: "Summarise the main argument of this passage in one or two sentences.", model: "Cheap, fast fashion has hidden environmental costs, such as excessive water use, pollution and waste, so people are being encouraged to buy fewer, longer-lasting clothes instead.", evidence: "This draws together the problems and proposed solution described across the whole passage." },
    { type: "mc", skill: "language", q: "What is suggested by the phrase \"hidden behind a low price tag\"?", options: ["The true cost of clothing includes environmental damage not shown in the shop price", "Clothes are always too expensive", "Prices are always clearly displayed", "The environmental cost is very small"], correct: 0, evidence: "The passage argues there is a \"true environmental cost\" that isn't reflected in what a customer actually pays." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "rc-y6-03", year: "Y6", genre: "Adventure", title: "The Descent",
  text: "The abseiling instructor's voice crackled reassuringly through the radio clipped to Zara's harness, though from ninety metres up, reassurance felt like a distant, almost laughable concept. Below her, the canyon floor looked impossibly small, a thin ribbon of river winding between sheer rock walls streaked orange and grey, exactly the view she had seen in photographs online for months beforehand but never quite believed she would face herself, no matter how many times she scrolled past them. Her hands, slick with sweat inside her gloves, gripped the rope with a fierceness that surprised even her, knuckles pale beneath the fabric. Somewhere far below, she could just make out her older brother's tiny figure, already waiting at the bottom and no doubt filming the whole thing on his phone. Slowly, deliberately, she leaned back into the harness and let herself drop the first few feet, forcing her mind to focus on nothing but the rhythm: feed the rope, walk the wall, breathe.",
  questions: [
    { type: "mc", skill: "retrieval", q: "How high up was Zara, according to the passage?", options: ["Nine metres", "Ninety metres", "Nine hundred metres", "Nineteen metres"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"Though from ninety metres up, reassurance felt like a distant concept.\"" },
    { type: "written", skill: "inference", q: "What does the phrase \"a fierceness that surprised even her\" suggest about Zara?", model: "It suggests that Zara found unexpected strength or determination within herself in this frightening moment, more than she thought she had.", evidence: "The word \"surprised\" implies she didn't expect to grip so tightly, showing she discovered inner resolve under pressure." },
    { type: "mc", skill: "vocabulary", q: "What does \"reassuringly\" mean in this passage?", options: ["In a way that causes more fear", "In a way meant to comfort or calm someone", "Very loudly and harshly", "Without any emotion at all"], correct: 1, evidence: "The instructor's voice being \"reassuringly\" calm fits with trying to help Zara feel less afraid." },
    { type: "written", skill: "prediction", q: "Based on the final sentence, what do you think Zara's approach to the rest of the descent will be?", model: "She will likely try to stay calm and focused by concentrating on repeating the same simple steps (feeding the rope, walking the wall, breathing) rather than thinking about the height.", evidence: "The passage ends with her deliberately narrowing her focus to \"nothing but the rhythm\" of these three actions." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "rc-y6-04", year: "Y6", genre: "Mystery", title: "The Anonymous Benefactor",
  text: "For three years, an unsigned donation had arrived at the community centre every December, always exactly enough to cover the winter fuel costs for struggling local families. Nobody knew who sent it, though theories abounded -- a wealthy former resident, a guilty local business, even a retired teacher fondly remembered by half the town for her generosity over the decades. Every December, the centre's staff would gather round the envelope and debate the possibilities all over again, none of them any closer to an answer. It was only when the centre's accountant noticed the donations always arrived the day after the same elderly gentleman's monthly pension payment that a quieter, more modest explanation began to seem far more likely than anyone had previously imagined -- and, once she thought it through properly and quietly double-checked the dates, far more moving too than any of the wealthy-stranger theories had ever been.",
  questions: [
    { type: "mc", skill: "retrieval", q: "How long had the anonymous donations been arriving?", options: ["One year", "Three years", "Ten years", "Six months"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"For three years, an unsigned donation had arrived at the community centre every December.\"" },
    { type: "written", skill: "inference", q: "What does the accountant's discovery suggest about who the donor might be?", model: "It suggests the donor is likely the elderly gentleman himself, giving away part of his own modest pension rather than being a wealthy stranger as people assumed.", evidence: "The timing lining up between the donations and his pension payment hints strongly that he is the source, especially combined with the phrase \"quieter, more modest explanation\"." },
    { type: "mc", skill: "language", q: "What does \"theories abounded\" mean in this passage?", options: ["There was only one theory", "Many different theories and guesses existed", "Nobody had any theories at all", "The theories were all proven true"], correct: 1, evidence: "The passage lists several different guesses (a wealthy resident, a guilty business, a retired teacher), matching \"abounded\" meaning \"existed in large numbers\"." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "rc-y6-05", year: "Y6", genre: "Nature", title: "The Return of the Beavers",
  text: "After being hunted to extinction in Britain centuries ago, largely for their fur and a musky substance once used in medicine, beavers have recently been reintroduced to several carefully managed river sites, with remarkable results. Beavers are known as a \"keystone species\" because their dam-building dramatically reshapes entire habitats, creating wetland areas that support countless other species, from dragonflies to otters, within just a few years of their arrival. Their dams also slow the flow of floodwater during heavy rain, reducing flood risk for communities downstream, sometimes dramatically so during the worst winter storms. What was once seen as a simple conservation project has increasingly been recognised as a surprisingly effective, low-cost approach to managing entire ecosystems, and similar reintroductions are now being seriously considered across other parts of Europe, with several early trial sites already reporting genuinely encouraging results for both local wildlife and the nearby flood defences alike.",
  questions: [
    { type: "mc", skill: "retrieval", q: "What is a beaver described as, due to its major effect on habitats?", options: ["An invasive species", "A keystone species", "An endangered predator", "A migratory species"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"Beavers are known as a 'keystone species' because their dam-building dramatically reshapes entire habitats.\"" },
    { type: "written", skill: "summarising", q: "Summarise the main benefits of reintroducing beavers, according to the passage.", model: "Beavers create wetland habitats that support many other species and their dams help reduce flood risk downstream, making their reintroduction beneficial for whole ecosystems.", evidence: "Both benefits (supporting other species and reducing flood risk) are directly stated in the passage." },
    { type: "mc", skill: "vocabulary", q: "What does \"reintroduced\" mean in this passage?", options: ["Removed permanently from an area", "Brought back to an area after being absent", "Studied scientifically for the first time", "Hunted again after protection ended"], correct: 1, evidence: "Beavers were \"hunted to extinction\" and then \"reintroduced\", meaning brought back after being gone." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "rc-y6-06", year: "Y6", genre: "History-inspired", title: "The Printing Revolution",
  text: "Before the mid-1400s, every single book in Europe had to be copied out entirely by hand, a painstaking process that could take a skilled scribe months or even years to complete a single manuscript, and a single mistake could mean starting an entire page again. When Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing press using movable metal type, arranging individual letters that could be reused for different pages, he transformed this reality almost overnight. Suddenly, dozens or even hundreds of identical copies of a text could be produced in the time it once took to create one, dramatically reducing the cost of each individual book. This explosion in the availability of books helped spread new ideas across Europe faster than ever before, fundamentally reshaping how knowledge itself could travel, from scientific discoveries to religious debates that had once taken many long generations to reach ordinary people living far away from the great cities.",
  questions: [
    { type: "mc", skill: "retrieval", q: "What did books require before Gutenberg's invention, according to the passage?", options: ["Being printed digitally", "Being copied out entirely by hand", "Being translated first", "Being read aloud only"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"Before the mid-1400s, every single book in Europe had to be copied out entirely by hand.\"" },
    { type: "written", skill: "inference", q: "Why might the spread of new ideas have sped up after the invention of the printing press?", model: "Because many more copies of a book could be made much faster than before, ideas written in books could reach far more people in far less time.", evidence: "The passage states the invention meant \"hundreds of identical copies of a text could be produced\" quickly, directly enabling ideas to \"spread... faster than ever before\"." },
    { type: "mc", skill: "language", q: "What does the phrase \"almost overnight\" suggest about the impact of the printing press?", options: ["The change happened extremely gradually over centuries", "The change happened remarkably quickly compared to what came before", "The printing press was only used at night", "Nothing actually changed"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"Almost overnight\" is a figurative phrase emphasising how sudden and dramatic the change felt compared to the slow hand-copying process before it." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "rc-y6-07", year: "Y6", genre: "Science-themed", title: "The Mystery of Bird Migration",
  text: "Every autumn, countless bird species undertake extraordinary journeys, sometimes travelling thousands of miles between breeding grounds and warmer wintering areas, often returning to the exact same nesting site the following spring. Exactly how they navigate such vast distances with such accuracy has puzzled scientists for generations, particularly since many young birds complete their very first migration without any adult guiding them at all. Current research suggests birds may use a remarkable combination of methods: the position of the sun and stars, recognisable landmarks such as coastlines and mountain ranges, and even an internal sense of the Earth's magnetic field, allowing them to detect direction rather like an inbuilt compass. Despite decades of study using tiny tracking devices, many details of this ability remain only partly understood, and new migration routes are still being discovered today as satellite technology becomes smaller and more affordable to use on even the smallest of birds.",
  questions: [
    { type: "mc", skill: "retrieval", q: "According to the passage, what internal sense might birds use to navigate?", options: ["A sense of smell", "An internal sense of the Earth's magnetic field", "A sense of taste", "An internal clock only"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"An internal sense of the Earth's magnetic field, allowing them to detect direction rather like an inbuilt compass.\"" },
    { type: "written", skill: "summarising", q: "Summarise the possible methods birds may use to navigate during migration.", model: "Birds may navigate using the position of the sun and stars, visible landmarks like coastlines and mountains, and an internal sense of the Earth's magnetic field.", evidence: "All three methods are listed directly in the middle of the passage." },
    { type: "mc", skill: "vocabulary", q: "What does \"extraordinary\" mean in this passage?", options: ["Very ordinary and unremarkable", "Highly unusual and impressive", "Extremely short", "Completely accidental"], correct: 1, evidence: "The journeys are described as \"extraordinary\" given they can be \"thousands of miles\" with great accuracy, suggesting something impressive." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "rc-y6-08", year: "Y6", genre: "Biography-style", title: "The Persistent Cartographer",
  text: "For over a decade, cartographer Beatrice Lin worked largely unrecognised on a single, painstaking project: an accurate map of a remote mountain range that official surveys had always got slightly wrong, sometimes by no more than a few dozen metres. Colleagues frequently questioned why she dedicated so much time and her own limited funding to such a seemingly minor correction, when there were more celebrated projects she could have pursued instead. Beatrice's answer never wavered: inaccurate maps, however small the error, had once led rescue teams to search the wrong valley entirely during a genuine emergency, costing precious time that a missing hiker could not afford to lose. When her corrected map was finally published, mountain rescue services adopted it within weeks, and lives it likely saved were never publicly counted, exactly as Beatrice herself always insisted they shouldn't be, since to her the map correcting itself was reward enough for all those years of quiet, careful work.",
  questions: [
    { type: "mc", skill: "retrieval", q: "What had once happened because of an inaccurate map, according to the passage?", options: ["A team won an award", "Rescue teams searched the wrong valley during an emergency", "A mountain was renamed", "Nobody used maps anymore"], correct: 1, evidence: "\"Inaccurate maps... had once led rescue teams to search the wrong valley entirely during a genuine emergency.\"" },
    { type: "written", skill: "inference", q: "What does Beatrice's insistence that the lives saved \"shouldn't be counted\" suggest about her character?", model: "It suggests she is modest and motivated by genuinely helping people rather than seeking praise or recognition for her work.", evidence: "She worked \"largely unrecognised\" for over a decade and specifically didn't want credit tallied up, showing humility rather than a desire for fame." },
    { type: "mc", skill: "language", q: "What does the word \"painstaking\" suggest about Beatrice's work?", options: ["It was quick and effortless", "It required great care, effort and patience", "It was done carelessly", "It was completed by accident"], correct: 1, evidence: "Working for \"over a decade\" on a single detailed correction fits with \"painstaking\", meaning done with great care and effort." }
  ]
}

